# KaiSpace Client Environment Variables.
# Copy to client/.env (or .env.local — both gitignored) and fill in. Vite only
# exposes vars prefixed VITE_, and it INLINES them into the built bundle —
# never put anything here that must stay secret from the browser.

# ── Server ────────────────────────────────────
# Where the Socket.IO/REST server lives. Leave unset for local and LAN play on
# the DEFAULT port: the client then derives it from the page's own hostname
# (see src/services/serverUrl.ts) and vite.config.ts proxies to :3001.
#
# Set it when the server is somewhere else — behind a tunnel, a real
# deployment, or simply on another port. It drives BOTH the runtime socket
# URL and the dev-server proxy, deliberately: when those two disagreed, the
# page loaded fine and only login failed, with "Invalid email or password"
# — because the proxy was reaching a DIFFERENT backend (another checkout of
# this app on the same machine, with its own database) where the account
# genuinely didn't exist. Nothing errors in that situation; the request just
# lands somewhere else.
#
# Must match server/.env's PORT. Running a second checkout of this app
# alongside this one is exactly when you need it:
# VITE_SERVER_URL="http://localhost:3002"

# ── TURN (WebRTC relay) ───────────────────────
# Optional. Unset = STUN only, which is fine on a shared LAN but fails on
# office/campus WiFi and symmetric-NAT mobile networks: the peer connects,
# chat and movement work, and the camera just silently never appears.
# TURN relays the media when a direct path is impossible.
#
# All THREE must be set together — a TURN entry without credentials is
# rejected by the browser. With any of them missing the app falls back to
# STUN only, so a half-filled config degrades cleanly instead of breaking.
#
# VITE_TURN_URL accepts several comma-separated URLs; listing both the UDP
# and the TCP/TLS :443 endpoint matters, because the TCP one is what gets
# through firewalls that drop UDP outright.
#
# Get free credentials at https://www.metered.ca/tools/openrelay/ (free tier
# is ~20 GB relay/month — enough to prove it works, not enough for daily
# office use; production needs a paid TURN service).
#
# NOTE: these end up readable in the browser. That is inherent to browser
# TURN, not a mistake here — keeping them in env means they can be rotated
# or revoked without a code change, and never enter git.
# VITE_TURN_URL="turn:xxx.metered.live:443,turns:xxx.metered.live:443?transport=tcp"
# VITE_TURN_USERNAME=""
# VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=""

# ── RustDesk self-hosted relay (Minta Bantuan Remote) ─────────────────────
# Optional. Unset = the "Minta Bantuan Remote" feature's setup panel doesn't
# show a copyable one-time RustDesk client config — everything else about
# the feature still works, since KaiSpace itself never talks to RustDesk
# directly (see server/src/socket/remoteHelpHandler.ts's own doc comment).
# These are the values every user's RustDesk client needs to be pointed at
# ONCE (Settings -> Network -> ID/Relay Server + Key) so a session's ID
# actually resolves to a server both sides can reach, instead of relying on
# RustDesk's public default relay (which office/campus firewalls often
# block). VITE_RUSTDESK_KEY is the id_ed25519.pub PUBLIC key hbbs generates
# on first run (see server/scripts or the deploy notes for how it was
# retrieved) — public by design, not a secret, but still env-configured so
# it can be rotated without a code change if the relay is ever redeployed.
# VITE_RUSTDESK_ID_SERVER=""
# VITE_RUSTDESK_RELAY_SERVER=""
# VITE_RUSTDESK_KEY=""
